                <pb facs="0714" n="598" sameAs="frus:frus1923v01/pg598"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">493.11/947: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Schurman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1923-12-28T15:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">December 28,
                                1923—3 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received December 28—8:02
                        a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>426. My despatch no. 1905, November 5. Lengthy identical note dated December 26
                    just received from Chinese Foreign Office refuses to accept the views expressed
                    in the joint notes of February 24th and November 5th [<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >3d</hi>] on the principal ground that the provision of law [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">protocol?</hi>] fixes gold equivalent of the indemnity
                    in the respective currencies now [<hi rendition="#italic">not?</hi>] in gold
                    specie. Many other arguments advanced. Chinese Foreign Office will publish the
                    note today. It will be discussed by the Ministers concerned. Copy by
                        mail.<anchor n="23" xml:id="d455-f1"/></p>
                <closer>For the Minister:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                    >Bell</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
